# Implement read-only mailbox workflows

## Goal

Ship useful read access before any mailbox mutation is enabled.

## Required work

- Implement mailbox and label listing.
- Implement paginated message and thread search.
- Implement message, header, and attachment retrieval.
- Implement conversation retrieval with explicit mapping between Proton
  conversations and provider-neutral threads.
- Bound body and attachment sizes and sanitize filenames.
- Preserve stable identifiers across pagination and reconnects.

## Current evidence

- Mailbox/label catalog metadata can be captured passively and retained as
  immutable in-memory snapshots, then paged with authenticated
  account/generation/query-bound cursors without rereading Proton during
  continuation. Public transport DTO fields remain undefined by the frozen
  contract, so no wire shape is invented.
- Exact single-message `mail/v4/messages/{messageID}` responses can now be
  reduced to bounded identity/envelope/read-state/label/MIME and attachment
  descriptor metadata while dropping body, headers, passwords, cryptographic
  packets, and attachment bytes. The managed browser can passively wait for the
  next app-owned detail GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or
  injecting
  a provider call, with clean timeout and ambiguity handling. This still does
  not satisfy targeted `get_message`: current WebClients may use cached message
  body without a network fetch, and plaintext body/header plus attachment
  decryption evidence remains open.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every path is classified read-only by the tool contract.
- Search, pagination, Unicode, MIME, and malformed-message cases are tested.
- Attachment traversal and oversized outputs fail safely.
- Logs contain identifiers and timing only, never content.
- The primary web UI adapter passes every read workflow; no paid Bridge or
  direct-API client identity is an unstated prerequisite.

## Evidence

- Synthetic mailbox fixtures
- Contract and adapter integration tests
